16 0 obj
<< /A 38 0 R /Alt (\(A\) A kratom tree in a field under a clear blue sky. \(B\) A pile of fresh kratom leaves. \(C\) Graphs displaying chemical analysis data with multiple peaks and annotations for different kratom compounds. \(D\) Grid of images showing kratom leaves at different withering durations and drying temperatures, ranging from negative forty degrees Celsius to eighty degrees Celsius over zero to seventy-two hours. \(E\) Close-up of kratom leaves with drying temperatures of twenty-five degrees Celsius and sixty degrees Celsius, observed over zero and twelve hours.) /K 0 /P 9 0 R /Pg 26 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
17 0 obj
<< /A 39 0 R /Alt (Bar chart comparing mitragynine content \(%w/w\) in two studies. Study I \(Hawaii\) shows analysis across different withering durations and drying temperatures for summer and winter. Study II \(MR-Malaysian\) covers the same variables without summer/winter differentiation, noting analysis was not done at some points. Error bars and letters indicate statistical differences.) /K 0 /P 9 0 R /Pg 29 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
18 0 obj
<< /A 40 0 R /Alt (Flowchart illustrating the biosynthetic pathways of mitragynine and related compounds from the shikimic acid and MEP pathways. Tryptophan converts into tryptamine, forming strictosidine with secologanin. Subsequent steps lead to corynantheidine derivatives, culminating in compounds like mitragynine, speciogynine, paynantheine, and ajmalicine. Enzymes like STR and SGD are indicated, with arrows denoting progression.) /K 0 /P 9 0 R /Pg 34 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
